Z||000010||rogergarrison||12-04-2001||05:37 PM||||Ive got a Panasonic 3.5 disk cam, similar to Sony Matvica. I love it. I have 2 top line 35 MM cams that I never ever use. 1 roll of film in 3 years. I use the digital everywhere. it holds 1200 pics in normal, 80 some in high res. on each 25 cent disk. In high res, there very good, normal is more than passable. Thats what i use to post all my pics, and never pay $15-$20 to develop film again.......ever. I just keep the other cams in cars to take in an emergency (but the batteries are prob dead). Cost me $399.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Neal||12-04-2001||06:05 PM||||so, if you want to get pics printed at a photolab you need 3.1 megapixel?<P>whats the difference between optical zoom and digital zoom?<P>any other info i should know before i go shopping, ive wanted one for a while but dont know alot about the things||||reg||1
Z||000012||avengador1||12-04-2001||07:35 PM||||Digital zoom just makes the pixels larger.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Formula88||12-04-2001||07:41 PM||||Optical zoom is magnifying the image with optics, like a telephoto lens, no loss in resolution.  Digital zoom just blows up the image like magnifying an image on your PC, with loss in resoltion.<P>From what I hear 3.1MP is required for any kind of large size printing, i.e. 5x7, 8x10.  2.1 is ok for snapshot 4x6, but gets grainy above that.||||reg||1
Z||000014||grinthock||12-04-2001||08:23 PM||||Right now I am working at the local electronic shop we carry 12 different ones.<P>Based on the Mpixel's and other features get the Kodak 3600 it's the best bang for the buck!!!<P>Basicall it's the same as the 3500 but it also does mini video's WITH sound!  spend the little extra it's worth it.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Neal||12-04-2001||08:40 PM||||So really digital zoom isn't that important, I can just crop and zoom on the PC, right?<P>optical zoom is exactly what I thought, I do have some experience with cameras after all.<P>so if 3.1MP will make a quality 5X7 print why is it 1200X1600 image in the computer monitor?<P>Also is it any extra money to develop digital pics vs. standard 35mm film?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Standard||12-04-2001||10:06 PM||||Hey Grinthock,<P>How much does the kodak 3600 cost?  That sounds like exactly what I'm looking for.. don't care about anything over 2 MPixel and it has to have a movie recording mode.  Any other good choices out there?  Oh yeah, and it has to be USB..<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Nashco||12-05-2001||01:01 AM||||I bought a Sony DSC-S30 the other day on ebay for 220 bucks.  It has 3x optical zoom and 6x with digital zoom.  It is 1.6 megapixel maximum and has an awesome swiveling LCD screen.  I'm very pleased with it, especially with the adjustable aperture and the 80 megabyte memory card I bought for it.  I can take all kinds of pictures with effects and stuff, and the memory stick will hold over 100 of the highest resolution photos.<P>Oh, and 1.3 megapixel (1280x960) is very good quality, for me.  The only time I have wanted better quality was on close ups.  I use a set of close up lenses (7x and 10x or together for 17x) to get VERY clear shots.  My 3x optical paired with the 17x of the lenses will take shots literally ON an item with crystal clear viewing.<P>Oh, it also uses a long-lasting lithium battery, will last about three hours worth of picture taking on a full charge, only about 1.5-2 if you're using flash.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000018||Brandon86SE||12-05-2001||01:30 AM||||Alrighty, this is so my topic!  I work in the Computer department at the Staples by Chinook Center in Calgary, and sell dozens of these things!  I agree with Grinthock on the Kodak's, they have traditionally been a good camera, and they have some great new ones out.  Personally I have a HP 618C, amazing picture quality!  Plus, they're a damn good price right now.  Even similar quality can be found with the HP 315 which Flashy (Steve) has.  The main difference is the Optical zoom, which is a true zoom.  Digital is just cropping an image and blowing it up, nothing special.<P>Neal - Come see me!  I'll be at work Thursday 4:30-9:00, and Sunday 12-6:00, those are my next 2 shifts, and would love to help ya out.<P>Brandon||||reg||1
Z||000019||Adrift||12-05-2001||01:37 AM||||I have a kodak DC280 - its unbelievable... I paid $600 CAN for it about 2 years ago so you should find it for under $200 USD these days.. AWESOME CAMERA. Period.||||reg||1
Z||000020||84Bill||12-05-2001||09:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Adrift:<BR><B>I have a kodak DC280 - its unbelievable... I paid $600 CAN for it about 2 years ago so you should find it for under $200 USD these days.. AWESOME CAMERA. Period.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have the same camera and it really rocks! Kodak seems to get good ratings for it's cameras. BTW take the digiatl zoom and ignore it! It is useless ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Patrick's Dad||12-05-2001||10:14 AM||||3.1Mp is acceptable as a 5x7 or 8x10, no more. 4Mp is pretty good as an 8x10, but even the 5.2Mp Minolta does not compete with film.<P>Two issues: resolution and dynamic range.<P>The digital cameras are catching up on resolution. They need to get to 7-8Mp to equal consumer films for resolution, over 10Mp to equal the best consumer and some pro films.<P>Dynamic Range is an issue that is being worked on. Right now, you can't improve much on highlight and shadow detail on a digital camera. Even the 5Mp machines will have muddy shadows and blown out highlights. As a secondary issue to DR is chip noise. Longer exposures (low light) mean more heat build up on the chip, which means interference from one pixel to another. It will take a while to deal with this, but Fuji (SuperCCD) and Minolta (Advanced heat sinking in the Dimage 5 & 7) have made strides. This is also why the Nikon and Olympus cameras' "bulb" settings shut down after 8-16 seconds. Not important for most, but I know people who take pics through a telescope.<P>I'll keep my Leica M6TTL for a while longer....||||reg||1
Z||000022||Formula88||12-05-2001||10:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Neal:<BR><B>so if 3.1MP will make a quality 5X7 print why is it 1200X1600 image in the computer monitor?<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Because the dot pitch on your monitor is much higher than that of a photograph.  The typical monitor is 72dpi, but printers can print color pix up to 1440dpi.<BR>||||reg||1
